var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"numerics/parallelism/#Parallel-decomposition","page":"Parallel decomposition","text":"CompactLES uses a three-dimensional Cartesian MPI process grid. Each rank owns a rectangular interior block plus halos. Shared-memory threads operate within that block, while MPI exchanges halos and couples the compact line solves.","title":"Parallel decomposition"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/parallelism/#Process-grid-rules","page":"Parallel decomposition","text":"Numerics(dims=nothing) asks MPI to distribute ranks over active dimensions. Set dims=(p₁,p₂,p₃) to control the layout explicitly. The following invariants are enforced:\n\nprod(dims) equals the MPI communicator size;\ncollapsed dimensions (n_global[d] == 1) have dims[d] == 1; and\neach local extent satisfies the selected scheme's minimum size.\n\nWith the default filter, a useful preflight bound is n_global[d] ≥ 9dims[d] for each decomposed direction.","title":"Process-grid rules"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/parallelism/#Why-the-compact-solve-is-collective","page":"Parallel decomposition","text":"A compact derivative is globally coupled along each grid line. Splitting that line across ranks does not turn it into independent local derivatives. CompactLES factors the local banded blocks, forms a reduced interface system, solves that system on the one-dimensional subcommunicator, and back-substitutes locally.\n\nConsequently, every rank in a directional subcommunicator must enter an operator application. Boundary code must not return early on ranks that do not own the physical face until all collective derivative calls are complete.","title":"Why the compact solve is collective"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/parallelism/#Threads","page":"Parallel decomposition","text":"Line and point loops use shared-memory threads only above THREAD_MIN_WORK, which defaults to 32768 units of work and can be overridden with CL_THREAD_MIN_WORK. This avoids task-launch overhead on small local blocks. MPI is initialized with the :funneled thread level; MPI calls remain outside threaded regions.","title":"Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/parallelism/#Launcher-portability","page":"Parallel decomposition","text":"Do not hard-code mpiexec. MPI.jl selects either its artifact-provided MPI or the configured system implementation:\n\nusing MPI\nMPI.mpiexec() do mpiexec\n    run(`$mpiexec -n 8 $(Base.julia_cmd()) --project=. -t 2 your_case.jl`)\nend\n\nScaling results depend on Julia thread placement, physical core topology, and the MPI implementation. Record those alongside timings.","title":"Launcher portability"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/operators/#Operators-and-decomposition","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"","title":"Operators and decomposition"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/operators/#Scheme-definitions","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"","title":"Scheme definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/operators/#Decomposition-and-storage","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"","title":"Decomposition and storage"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/operators/#Directional-plans","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"","title":"Directional plans"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.ClosureRow","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"ClosureRow(lhs, rhs)\n\nOne low-edge closure row for a tridiagonal CompactScheme. lhs is the (subdiagonal, diagonal, superdiagonal) tuple and rhs contains weights on points counted inward from the edge. High-edge rows are mirrored automatically.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.ClosureRow"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.CompactScheme","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"CompactScheme(name, alpha, a0, coeffs, symmetric, closures)\n\nTridiagonal compact operator definition. Antisymmetric schemes represent first derivatives and are scaled by the grid spacing when planned; symmetric schemes represent filters and include the center coefficient a0.\n\nUse lele_d1_6, pade_d1_4, or compact_filter unless defining a custom compact scheme.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.CompactScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.BandedClosureRow","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"BandedClosureRow(lhs, rhs)\n\nOne edge closure row for a BandedCompactScheme. lhs contains the full centered band and rhs contains weights counted inward from the edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.BandedClosureRow"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.BandedCompactScheme","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"BandedCompactScheme(name, q, lhs, a0, coeffs, symmetric, closures)\n\nCompact operator with left-hand-side half-bandwidth q. This is the pentadiagonal-capable counterpart of CompactScheme; the built-in lele_d1_10 is the usual entry point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.BandedCompactScheme"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.pade_d1_4","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"pade_d1_4()\n\nFourth-order Padé first derivative: α = 1/4, a = 3/2, with a third-order one-sided closure on the first row (the interior stencil is valid from row 2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.pade_d1_4"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.lele_d1_6","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"lele_d1_6()\n\nSixth-order tridiagonal first derivative (Lele 1992): α = 1/3, a = 14/9, b = 1/9, with third/fourth-order one-sided closures on the first two rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.lele_d1_6"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.lele_d1_10","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"lele_d1_10()\n\nTenth-order pentadiagonal first derivative (Lele 1992): β = 1/20, α = 1/2, a = 17/12, b = 101/150, c = 1/100 (consistency: a + b + c = 1 + 2α + 2β). Three closure rows are needed at closed edges (the interior RHS reaches ±3): the C6 third/fourth-order one-sided rows on rows 1–2 and the C6 tridiagonal interior row on row 3 — the usual boundary cascade, with local order reduction near walls. Requires halo width nhalo ≥ 3 (default nhalo = 4 is fine).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.lele_d1_10"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.compact_filter","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"compact_filter(alphaf=0.45)\n\nEighth-order Gaitonde–Visbal compact filter. alphaf ∈ (−0.5, 0.5) sets the strength (larger → weaker filtering). Near closed edges the first row is left unfiltered and rows 2–4 apply centered compact filters of order 2, 4, 6 with the same αf — the standard reduced-order boundary cascade.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.compact_filter"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.Decomp","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"Decomp(n_global, periodic; dims=nothing, n_halo=4)\n\nThree-dimensional Cartesian MPI decomposition of a structured global grid. dims is the process grid; when omitted MPI chooses it over active dimensions. Each rank owns n_local interior points and halo-padded storage. Dimensions with one global point are collapsed and are never decomposed.\n\nMost users receive a Decomp through setup. Construct one directly only when using the low-level field and compact-operator APIs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.Decomp"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.interior","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"CartesianIndices of the interior (halo-offset) block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.interior"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.field","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"Allocate a scalar field with halos (collapsed dims carry no padding).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.field"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.allocate_state","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"Allocate a conserved state Q(x,y,z,1:n_cons) with halos.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.allocate_state"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.exchange_halos!","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"exchange_halos!(f, decomp)\n\nFill the rank-boundary halos of scalar field f (sized nlocal .+ 2*nhalo) from neighboring ranks, all three dimensions. Sequential per dimension, so edge and corner halos come out filled without dedicated diagonal messages. Must be called from a serial (non-threaded) section.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.exchange_halos!"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.DirPlan","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"DirPlan\n\nDirectional execution plan for a tridiagonal CompactScheme. It binds the scheme to one dimension and decomposition, with prescaled coefficients, edge closures, distributed line solver, and reusable packed-line storage.\n\nPlans are constructed by setup; users normally pass them to apply_along! rather than constructing them directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.DirPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.BandPlan","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"BandPlan\n\nDirectional execution plan for a BandedCompactScheme, using the distributed banded line solver. It is the pentadiagonal-capable counterpart of DirPlan and is constructed by setup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.BandPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.apply_along!","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"apply_along!(out, plan, f, decomp)\n\nApply the compact operator of plan to field f along plan.dim, writing the result into the interior of out. f must have current rank-boundary halos.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.apply_along!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.filter_field!","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"filter_field!(f, solver; σf=1)\n\nApply the compact filter along all active dimensions of f in place, with a halo exchange before each directional pass; σf is the field's axis parity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.filter_field!"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/operators/#CompactLES.THREAD_MIN_WORK","page":"Operators and decomposition","text":"Minimum total work (in grid points) before a loop is worth handing to the thread pool. Tunable at runtime; the default was chosen from the table above, where 32^3 is roughly break-even and 48^3 is a clear win. Override with the CL_THREAD_MIN_WORK environment variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.THREAD_MIN_WORK"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"The frontend separates the physical problem from numerical and parallel choices. A Problem contains the domain, boundary conditions, pointwise initial condition, and current model selections. A Numerics contains the grid, compact schemes, CFL, filtering cadence, and process grid.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#A-minimal-periodic-problem","page":"Getting started","text":"Start Julia with the project environment and initialize MPI. setup also initializes MPI when necessary, but explicit initialization makes the requested thread level clear in applications.\n\nusing MPI\nMPI.Init(threadlevel=:funneled)\n\nusing CompactLES\n\nproblem = Problem(\n    name = \"one-dimensional wave\",\n    domain = ((0.0, 2π), (0.0, 1.0), (0.0, 1.0)),\n    bcs = ntuple(_ -> (PeriodicBC(), PeriodicBC()), 3),\n    ic = (x, y, z) -> Prim(\n        u = (0.05sin(x), 0.0, 0.0),\n        p = 1.0,\n        rho = 1.0,\n    ),\n)\n\nnumerics = Numerics(\n    n_global = (64, 1, 1),\n    deriv = lele_d1_6(),\n    filt = compact_filter(0.45),\n    cfl = 0.5,\n)\n\nsolver, Q = setup(problem, numerics)\nrun!(solver, Q; tfinal=0.01)\nnothing # hide\n\nA dimension with one global point is collapsed: it has no derivative, halo exchange, or decomposition. This makes the same three-dimensional frontend usable for one- and two-dimensional cases.","title":"A minimal periodic problem"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Reuse-and-observe-the-run","page":"Getting started","text":"Retain a Workspace when advancing in several calls:\n\nworkspace = Workspace(Q)\nrun!(solver, Q; workspace, tfinal=0.02)\nrun!(solver, Q; workspace, tfinal=0.03)\nnothing # hide\n\nThe callback runs after every complete step:\n\nrun!(solver, Q; workspace, tfinal=0.04,\n     callback=(solver, Q) -> begin\n         solver.step % 100 == 0 || return\n         @show solver.t dt_report(solver, Q)\n     end)\nnothing # hide\n\ncompute_dt applies the configured CFL to acoustic, diffusive, and collapsed-coordinate curvature rates. dt_report identifies the global limiting location and rate class when a timestep is unexpectedly small.","title":"Reuse and observe the run"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Run-with-MPI","page":"Getting started","text":"Ask MPI.jl for the launcher configured for the current environment:\n\nusing MPI\nMPI.mpiexec() do mpiexec\n    run(`$mpiexec -n 4 $(Base.julia_cmd()) --project=. examples/taylor_green.jl`)\nend\n\nSee Parallel decomposition for process-grid constraints.","title":"Run with MPI"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/frontend/#Problem-setup","page":"Problem setup","text":"","title":"Problem setup"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/frontend/#Pointwise-state-and-frontend","page":"Problem setup","text":"","title":"Pointwise state and frontend"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/frontend/#Coordinates","page":"Problem setup","text":"","title":"Coordinates"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.Prim","page":"Problem setup","text":"Prim(; u=(0,0,0), p, T_ion=NaN, rho=NaN, Y=(1.0,))\n\nPointwise primitive state: velocity components (physical, coordinate-aligned), pressure, mass fractions, and exactly one of temperature or density (the EOS supplies the other).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.Prim"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.Problem","page":"Problem setup","text":"Problem(; eos, transport, metric, domain, bcs, ic, name)\n\nPhysics and geometry only: EOS, transport, metric, coordinate domain as three (lo, hi) tuples, boundary conditions, and the IC function. Contains no resolution, scheme, or parallel information.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.Problem"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.Numerics","page":"Problem setup","text":"Numerics(; n_global, kwargs...)\n\nDiscretization and runtime choices: resolution, derivative/filter schemes, artificial-property constants, CFL, filter interval, process grid, halo width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.Numerics"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.setup","page":"Problem setup","text":"setup(prob, num) -> (solver, Q)\n\nConstruct the backend for prob at the resolution and schemes of num and return the solver plus the initialized conserved state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.setup"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.initialize!","page":"Problem setup","text":"initialize!(solver, Q, ic)\n\nFill the interior of Q from ic(x₁, x₂, x₃) -> Prim, evaluated at physical coordinates. The IC function never sees ranks, halos, offsets, or component layouts. IC functions should be pure (they are called from multiple threads).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.initialize!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.conserved_from_prim","page":"Problem setup","text":"conserved_from_prim(eos, pr) -> NTuple\n\nEOS contract: primitive → conserved (ρY₁..ρY_Ns, ρu, ρv, ρw, E). Implemented here for ideal mixtures; future EOS models supply their own method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.conserved_from_prim"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.tanh_blend","page":"Problem setup","text":"Smoothed step: 0 for x ≪ x0, 1 for x ≫ x0, tanh transition of width δ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.tanh_blend"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.xcoord","page":"Problem setup","text":"Physical coordinate of local interior index i along dimension d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.xcoord"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.gidx","page":"Problem setup","text":"Halo-offset CartesianIndex of local interior point (i, j, k).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.gidx"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.Stretch","page":"Problem setup","text":"Stretch(x, dxdξ)\n\nPer-dimension monotone grid mapping for stretched meshes: the computational coordinate ξ is uniform on [0, 1] and x(ξ) gives the physical coordinate, with dxdξ(ξ) its derivative. Composes with any base metric (the mapping Jacobian multiplies that dimension's scale factor), so clustered radial grids in cylindrical coordinates work the same way as clustered Cartesian ones. Stretched dimensions must be non-periodic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.Stretch"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frontend/#CompactLES.sine_cluster","page":"Problem setup","text":"sine_cluster(lo, hi, ξc, a)\n\nClosed-form interior clustering: x(ξ) = lo + L(ξ − (a/2π)[sin 2π(ξ−ξc) + sin 2πξc]), dx/dξ = L(1 − a cos 2π(ξ−ξc)). Spacing is smallest at ξ = ξc (fractional position of the cluster point), largest opposite it, with ratio (1+a)/(1−a); any 0 ≤ a < 1 keeps the map monotone. For boundary-layer-style one-sided clustering supply your own monotone map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.sine_cluster"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/runtime/#Runtime-and-output","page":"Runtime and output","text":"","title":"Runtime and output"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/runtime/#Right-hand-side-and-integration","page":"Runtime and output","text":"","title":"Right-hand side and integration"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/runtime/#Checkpoint-and-visualization-output","page":"Runtime and output","text":"","title":"Checkpoint and visualization output"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.Workspace","page":"Runtime and output","text":"Workspace(Q)\nWorkspace(solver)\n\nReusable low-storage RK stage arrays. Pass a retained workspace to run! or step! to avoid reallocating stage storage across calls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.Workspace"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.compute_rhs!","page":"Runtime and output","text":"compute_rhs!(solver, Q, dQ)\n\nEvaluate dQ/dt into the interior of dQ from the conserved state Q (boundary conditions should already be enforced on Q). Collapsed dimensions contribute no derivatives; the axis dimension routes through parity-folded plans with mirror-filled halos.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.compute_rhs!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.apply_bcs!","page":"Runtime and output","text":"Enforce all boundary conditions on the conserved state (active dims only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.apply_bcs!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.compute_dt","page":"Runtime and output","text":"compute_dt(solver, Q)\n\nCFL-limited timestep from the acoustic (|ud| + c) / (hd · scalefactor_d) rate and a diffusive rate built from the current (possibly artificial) transport coefficients, reduced over all ranks. Calls primitives! so the estimate is EOS-aware; artificial coefficients lag by one step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.compute_dt"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.dt_report","page":"Runtime and output","text":"dt_report(solver, Q)\n\nDiagnostic companion to compute_dt: returns a NamedTuple naming the global timestep limiter — (dt, rank, index, coords, dim, kind) where kind is :acoustic, :diffusive, or :curvature. Cheap enough to call every few hundred steps; the intended use is confirming whether a run is limited by the physics you care about or by the azimuthal spacing at a coordinate singularity (see the CFL notes in the README).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.dt_report"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.step!","page":"Runtime and output","text":"step!(solver, Q, dQ, du, dt)\n\nAdvance the conserved state by one RK45 step of size dt. dQ and du are caller-provided work arrays of the same shape as Q.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.step!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.run!","page":"Runtime and output","text":"run!(solver, Q; tfinal, nmax=typemax(Int), callback=nothing)\n\nAdvance to tfinal (or nmax steps), filtering the conserved variables every s.filter_interval steps and invoking callback(solver, Q) after each step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.run!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.filter_state!","page":"Runtime and output","text":"filter_state!(solver, Q)\n\nApply the compact filter to every conserved component along every active dimension, with batched per-dimension halo exchange and axis parity routing (ρur and ρuθ are odd across the axis; everything else is even).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.filter_state!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.save_checkpoint","page":"Runtime and output","text":"save_checkpoint(solver, Q, prefix)\n\nWrite the interior of Q plus (t, step) to prefix.rNNNN.ckpt, one file per rank. Collective only in the trivial sense that every rank writes; no communication is involved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.save_checkpoint"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.load_checkpoint!","page":"Runtime and output","text":"load_checkpoint!(solver, Q, prefix)\n\nRestore the interior of Q and (t, step) from prefix.rNNNN.ckpt, verifying grid, decomposition, and conserved-layout compatibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.load_checkpoint!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/runtime/#CompactLES.save_vtk","page":"Runtime and output","text":"save_vtk(solver, Q, prefix)\n\nWrite prefix.rNNNN.vtr per rank plus prefix.pvtr (rank 0) containing ρ, velocity, p, T_ion, and the mass fractions as Float32 point data on the physical rectilinear grid. Open the .pvtr in ParaView/VisIt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CompactLES.save_vtk"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/compact-operators/#Compact-operators","page":"Compact operators","text":"CompactLES represents a directional compact operator as an implicit banded relation between the output g and input f. For the tridiagonal family,\n\nalpha g_i-1 + g_i + alpha g_i+1\n= a_0 f_i + sum_m=1^M c_m\n  left(f_i+m mathbinpm f_i-mright)\n\nThe minus sign gives an antisymmetric first derivative; the plus sign and center coefficient a_0 give a symmetric filter. The right-hand side of a derivative is scaled by 1h when its directional plan is constructed.","title":"Compact operators"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/compact-operators/#Built-in-schemes","page":"Compact operators","text":"Constructor Interior scheme Implicit band Closed-edge behavior\npade_d1_4 fourth-order derivative tridiagonal third-order first row\nlele_d1_6 sixth-order derivative tridiagonal third/fourth-order first two rows\nlele_d1_10 tenth-order derivative pentadiagonal C6 closure cascade on the first three rows\ncompact_filter eighth-order filter tridiagonal identity, then second/fourth/sixth-order rows\n\nThe interior order is not the global order of a closed or folded domain. Boundary closures and singularity folds are approximately third order in the current convergence suite. Periodic domains expose the sixth- and tenth-order interior behavior.","title":"Built-in schemes"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/compact-operators/#Applying-a-scheme","page":"Compact operators","text":"Numerics accepts derivative and filter definitions directly:\n\nnum = Numerics(\n    n_global = (128, 64, 64),\n    deriv = lele_d1_10(),\n    filt = compact_filter(0.45),\n)\n\nsetup constructs one directional plan per active dimension. Each application packs all grid lines, evaluates the explicit right-hand side, solves the implicit system across every rank in that direction, and scatters the result. The y and z paths use a transposed line layout so their inner memory access remains contiguous.\n\nThe low-level apply_along! API requires current rank-boundary halos. filter_field! performs its own exchange before each directional filter pass.","title":"Applying a scheme"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/compact-operators/#Local-extent-constraints","page":"Compact operators","text":"Every rank-local extent must be large enough for both the implicit closure rows and the explicit stencil:\n\nC6 requires at least 5 points;\nC10 requires at least 7 points; and\nthe C8 filter requires at least 9 points.\n\nBecause filtering is enabled by default, 9 points per rank in every decomposed dimension is the practical minimum. A grid can therefore be valid for its derivative scheme and still be too small for filtering.","title":"Local extent constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/compact-operators/#Custom-schemes","page":"Compact operators","text":"CompactScheme and BandedCompactScheme are public so a custom coefficient set can use the same planning and distributed solves. Closure rows are specified at the low edge; the high edge is mirrored automatically, including the sign change for derivatives. New coefficient sets should add periodic and closed-domain convergence guards before being used in production.","title":"Custom schemes"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/validation/#Validation","page":"Validation","text":"The numerical gate is layered so failures point toward a specific part of the implementation.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/validation/#Serial-suite","page":"Validation","text":"julia --project=. test/runtests.jl\n\nThis covers compact operators, boundary closures, transposed sweeps, filters, coordinate folds, freestream preservation, conservation, EOS round-trips, boundary conditions, checkpoint/VTK I/O, and short end-to-end configurations.","title":"Serial suite"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/validation/#Convergence-guards","page":"Validation","text":"julia --project=. test/convergence.jl\n\nThe suite measures observed order rather than checking only a tolerance at one resolution. Current regression targets are approximately:\n\nCase Observed order\nperiodic C6 derivative 6.01\nperiodic C10 derivative 10.04\nC6 closed-wall closures 3.17\ncylindrical-axis odd/even folds 3.71 / 3.00\nresolved-angle axis fold 3.71\nspherical origin 2.99\n\nFor a change that is not intended to alter numerics, compare the printed error magnitudes as well as the fitted order. A moved digit can reveal a real discretization change before an order guard fails.","title":"Convergence guards"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/validation/#Distributed-suite","page":"Validation","text":"mpiexec -n 2 julia --project=. test/mpi_tests.jl\nmpiexec -n 4 julia --project=. test/mpi_tests.jl\nmpiexec -n 8 julia --project=. test/mpi_tests.jl\n\nThese runs exercise distributed tridiagonal and pentadiagonal solves, cross-rank halo exchange, off-rank folds, the discrete geometric conservation law, and telescoping flux conservation. Running several rank counts changes the ownership and interface patterns and is therefore materially stronger than one MPI smoke test.\n\nCI runs the serial suite on the minimum supported Julia, current Julia, and pre-release Julia. A separate job gates the convergence suite and all three MPI rank counts on current Julia. The documentation build is also a required job.","title":"Distributed suite"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/validation/#Performance-diagnostics","page":"Validation","text":"The scripts in bench/ are comparison tools rather than pass/fail tests. bench/jetcheck.jl and bench/audit.jl report inference and allocation sites; compare their counts before and after a performance-sensitive change. Do not sum overlapping entry-point counts.","title":"Performance diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"#CompactLES.jl","page":"Home","text":"CompactLES is a compressible large-eddy/direct-simulation solver built around high-order compact finite differences, compact filtering, low-storage RK time integration, and a distributed MPI line solve.\n\n(Image: Taylor–Green vorticity, a multicomponent shock-interface interaction, and a cylindrical converging shock)\n\nThe 64³ Taylor–Green run peaks at ε = 0.01265, t = 9.13, closely matching the pseudo-spectral reference peak ε = 0.01289, t = 8.86.\n\nThis manual deliberately documents the parts of the package that are already well established:\n\nthe Problem/Numerics frontend;\ncompact derivative and filter schemes;\nthe structured-grid decomposition and MPI execution model;\ntime stepping, diagnostics, checkpointing, and VTK output; and\nthe numerical regression suite.\n\nThe detailed physics model, boundary-condition catalogue, and coordinate-singularity machinery remain under active development. Their current design and implementation notes live in DESIGN.md, and the source remains authoritative while those interfaces evolve.","title":"CompactLES.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-begin","page":"Home","text":"Use Getting started to construct and advance a problem. Read Compact operators before changing spatial schemes, and Parallel decomposition before choosing a process grid. The Validation page records the checks that protect the established numerics.\n\nwarning: Research software\nCompactLES is research code under active development. Validate a configuration against an analytic or experimental reference before using its results for scientific conclusions.","title":"Where to begin"}]
}
